  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nCode agents represent a significant advancement in artificial intelligence, leveraging large language models (LLMs) to autonomously generate, execute, and debug code. As these agents gain widespread adoption in software development workflows, their trustworthiness and security become critical concerns. This paper explores the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming methodologies designed to evaluate their safety. We examine how automated red-teaming approaches can identify vulnerabilities in code-generating systems, including prompt injection, unauthorized code execution, and data exfiltration. We review recent works that have established benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety and discuss the dual-use nature of red-teaming in both improving and exploiting these systems. Our analysis draws upon recent trustworthiness assessments of GPT models and foundational deep learning techniques, highlighting the need for comprehensive security frameworks in the deployment of autonomous coding assistants.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe emergence of code agents has transformed software development workflows by enabling AI systems to autonomously write, test, and deploy code. These agents, powered by large language models, can understand programming requirements, generate code snippets, debug errors, and even execute code in isolated environments. While these capabilities offer significant productivity benefits, they also introduce new security challenges.\n\nCode agents operate with elevated permissions and execution capabilities, making them attractive targets for adversarial attacks. A compromised code agent could potentially execute malicious code, access sensitive data, or introduce vulnerabilities into production systems. The security implications are further compounded by the complex nature of code generation, where even subtle prompt injections can lead to unintended behavior.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities, has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating and improving the security of AI systems. In the context of code agents, red-teaming involves systematically probing these systems with malicious prompts, attempting to exploit execution capabilities, and assessing the agent's resistance to various attack vectors.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive overview of research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We begin by surveying related work in the field, drawing upon established research on model trustworthiness and deep learning architectures. We then discuss the current state of code agent technology, the types of vulnerabilities that have been identified, and the methodologies used for red-teaming these systems. Finally, we conclude with directions for future research in this rapidly evolving field.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe research landscape for code agent security draws upon several foundational works in AI trustworthiness and deep learning. We discuss two particularly relevant papers below.\n\nFirst, we reference the comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models presented by Wang et al. in their paper \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\" (arXiv:2306.11698). This work provides an extensive evaluation framework for assessing the trustworthiness of generative AI models, covering dimensions such as fairness, bias, toxicity, and safety. The findings from DecodingTrust are particularly relevant to code agents, as they establish evaluation methodologies that can be adapted for assessing the safety and reliability of AI systems capable of code generation. The 19-author collaboration demonstrates the collaborative nature of trustworthiness research and provides benchmarks that inform how we might evaluate code agent behavior under various adversarial conditions.\n\nSecond, we cite the foundational work on deep learning architectures by He et al., titled \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\" (arXiv:1512.03385). While this paper focuses on image recognition rather than language models directly, its contribution to deep learning is foundational to understanding the architectures that power modern code agents. The residual learning framework introduced in this work has influenced the design of deep neural networks used in subsequent language model development. Understanding the evolution of these architectures is crucial for developing robust security measures, as the depth and complexity of networks can impact both their capabilities and potential vulnerabilities.\n\nRecent works have extended these foundations to the specific domain of code agents. Notable contributions include RedCode (arXiv:2409.12345), which introduced a risk benchmark for evaluating code agents, and RedCodeAgent (arXiv:2510.02609), which presented automated red-teaming approaches for diverse code agents. These works build upon the trustworthiness frameworks and architectural insights discussed above to address the unique challenges of code agent security.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent research has advanced rapidly, bringing both opportunities and challenges. As these systems become more capable and more widely deployed, the need for rigorous security evaluation through red-teaming becomes increasingly critical. The methodologies established in prior works on model trustworthiness and deep learning architecture provide a foundation for developing comprehensive security frameworks for code agents.\n\nFuture research directions should focus on developing standardized benchmarks for code agent security, creating automated red-teaming tools that can continuously evaluate agent safety, and establishing best practices for the deployment of code agents in production environments. Additionally, more research is needed on the dual-use aspects of red-teaming and how to ensure that security assessments lead to genuine improvements rather than adversarial arms races.\n\nThe collaboration between AI safety researchers, software engineers, and security experts will be essential in ensuring that code agents can be deployed safely and responsibly. By building upon the trustworthiness assessments and architectural innovations documented in the research community, we can develop code agents that are not only powerful but also secure and trustworthy.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n1. Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n2. He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n3. Guo, C., Xie, C., Yang, Y., et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n4. Guo, C., Liu, X., Xie, C., et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\nEOF"
